            <ArticleTitle>The prevalence of chronic widespread pain in the general population.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>710-3</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To establish the prevalence of chronic widespread pain and associated symptoms in a general population sample.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Cross sectional postal survey of 2,034 adults in the north of England.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">The point prevalence of chronic widespread pain was 11.2%. The symptom was strongly associated with other somatic complaints and with measures of depression and anxiety.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In the general population, this cardinal symptom of fibromyalgia is common and identifies a group who are more likely also to report symptoms of fatigue and depression.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
